    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Comprised of two atria and two ventricles, the heart is responsible for the delivery of blood and nutrients to the different parts of the body. As a well-characterized chromatin-remodeling complex, INO80 is known to have a variety of functions in organisms, more recently including metabolic regulation. An endocardial knockout of Ino80, the ATPase subunit of the INO80 chromatin-remodeling complex, has been shown to result in reduced thickness in the myocardial wall of the heart during development. This is likely due to the disruption in the regulation of signaling pathways between the endocardium and myocardium. In light of this information, this study investigated the role of INO80 as a potential regulator of cardiac development, not just through regulation of signaling, but natively in the myocardium. In order to elucidate this function, this study characterized the myocardial Ino80 knockout in Mus musculus. This revealed a lack of a phenotype in the myocardial layer of the heart. This suggests that INO80 does not play a native role in the myocardium, rather its main function in cardiovascular development is signaling between the endocardial layer and the myocardial layer to promote development. This elucidates the importance in understanding the signaling pathways that INO80 plays during development, and narrows the potential function of INO80 in overall heart development.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
